we've got some important information for  couples this morning and it involves  fighting we are looking at how to win a  fight an argument if you will a recent  poll if you're wondering found that  americans fight with their spouse pretty  frequently about 30 of people say that  one or more times per week is normal for  them uh 28 say one or multiple times a  month  and i don't know these people but a  third of people say once or multiple  times per year of course it's not just  couples who get in arguments that's not  many arguments to be honest with you uh  or disagreements uh to help you win any  of them social psychologist heidi grant  is here with tips on how to get it done  and when you should back off and lay  down your arms and agree to disagree so  i was interested to read that you should  not in an argument focus on why you're  right  not right away right it actually turns  out to be that i think probably the  biggest mistake people make in an  argument with anyone we think that the  way we're going to win so so to speak is  to  just sort of pummel the other person  with all the reasons why we're right and  they're wrong and not surprisingly  what's wrong with that right it doesn't  actually work because actually all human  beings get a little defensive about  their beliefs their opinions their  preferences we want to feel like we're  right so it turns out there's a  technique that's really powerful it's  called a two-sided argument you actually  start by why the other person is right  right what's legitimate about your point  of view what's valid what's at least  understandable and if i do that right i  start by validating your perspective it  turns out you're much more open to mine  because it makes people less defensive  and then they can finally hear you right  so if you start by validating the other  person then follow up with why you're  right you're much more likely to  persuade them to come to your side  gail so you say i see why you feel that  way exactly and ideally  and you don't really see why they feel  that way i remember once in marital  counseling the guy says to me gail do  you want peace or do you want to be  right and i said i want both  he said well it doesn't work that way  and so i mean all this sounds really  good but in the heat of battle it's very  hard to validate i think you're  absolutely right and you said something  really important it's the heat of battle  so sometimes when we're going into these  conversations we are feeling a lot of  emotion and so being able to do that  sort of perspective taking where you do  understand where the other person is  coming from is quite hard  one simple thing anyone can do actually  at any time when you're feeling a lot of  strong emotion is what psychologists  call labeling it takes just a moment you  pause and think to yourself what is it  that i'm feeling i'm feeling anxious i'm  feeling angry i'm feeling frustrated i'm  feeling defensive and simply labeling  your emotion there's a lot of research  that shows it kind of brings it down to  a manageable level from there you can  actually think all right am i being fair  to this other person can i take their  perspective have a better conversation  speaking of labeling how do you label a  win what is a winner good question um i  mean i think the win losing frame is a  really bad way to approach any argument  i think really the goal should always be  you know ideally persuading them to  change to your point of view and at  minimum being understood i think often  in a relationship in particular if you  can get to a place where you feel there  was real mutual understanding that's its  own kind of win that has a lot of  lasting positive effects i see a ring on  your finger do you use these techniques  i do indeed um i'm sure my husband is  watching right now  and it's funny you know one of the  things that we just actually had this is  throwing shoes at the tv you may be  actually wondering now why i have 